Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.189 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  how could a moth possibly come out on  top in a dog fight with a bat a new  study shows that Mars aren't exactly  pushovers when pitted against their  nightly tormentors researchers believe  one of the reasons silk moths in the  Saturnian family stand a fighting chance  might have something to do with the  shape of their wings when it comes to  hunting moths and other flying insects  the most powerful tool in a bats arsenal  is its ability to use sonar to pick  targets out of the air however  researchers have found that the long  elaborate tails on silk moths hind wings  help to deflect the bats own arm like a  built-in decoy researchers found four  distinct wing types related to a silk  moths ability to escape bat attacks to  test the effectiveness of each wing type  they altered the profiles of three types  of silk moths to resemble the wing  shapes it faced off the modified moths  with big brown bats in a flight room and  captured the results on high-speed  cameras researchers found that the  larger the hind wing and longer the tail  the better the moths chances of escaping  bats on the hunt  since bats only eat a moths body and not  its wings that's what they look for when  hunting the tails offer multiple body  like targets tricking striking bats and  researchers say that the bats weren't  very effective at fighting away or on  the moths defense even after facing the  same types of hind wing shapes for  months bats still couldn't tell the  difference between a tasty moth morsel  and its tail driven decoy score one for  the underdog or moth  [Music]

